{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport urllib.request\nu='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mims-harvard/ToolUniverse/main/skills/tooluniverse-phylogenetics/SKILL.md'\ndata=urllib.request.urlopen(u,timeout=20).read().decode().splitlines()\nfor i in range(0,160): print(f'{i+1:4}: {data[i]}')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"   1: ---\n   2: name: tooluniverse-phylogenetics\n   3: description: Phylogenetic analysis — de novo multiple sequence alignment (Clustal Omega/MUSCLE/MAFFT via EBI_msa_align) and neighbour-joining/UPGMA tree building (EBI_build_phylogenetic_tree) from your own sequences, plus tree analysis, treeness, saturation (PhyKIT), parsimony-informative sites, alignment gap analysis, DVMC, long-branch detection, BUSCO orthologs. Uses PhyKIT, Biopython, DendroPy. Use to align a set of sequences, build a tree from sequences or an alignment, or for phylogenetic tree QC, multi-gene phylogenomics, evolutionary-rate analysis, and comparative-genomics studies.\n   4: disable-model-invocation: true\n   5: ---\n   6: \n   7: # Phylogenetics and Sequence Analysis\n   8: \n   9: ## RULE ZERO — Check for pre-computed results FIRST\n  10: \n  11: Before following any instruction below, scan the data folder for:\n  12: - **`scogs_fungi.zip` / `scogs_animals.zip`** (BUSCO single-copy ortholog phylogenetics) → these contain the pre-computed alignments (`*.faa.mafft.clipkit`) and trees (`*.faa.mafft.clipkit.treefile`) from the original analysis. **Use these directly with PhyKIT** (see \"BUSCO scogs questions\" below). Re-running BUSCO → MAFFT → IQ-TREE from `*.busco.zip` files takes 1–6 hours AND produces slightly different numbers due to seed/version drift.\n  13: - `*_executed.ipynb` → read with `tu run read_executed_notebook '{\"data_folder\":\"<path>\",\"search\":\"<keyword>\"}'` and cite its cell outputs as the authoritative answer\n  14: - Pre-computed result files (CSV/TSV with names like `*results*`, `*tree*`, `*phykit*`, `*saturation*`, `*treeness*`) → read directly and report the requested value\n  15: - Canonical analysis scripts (`analysis.R`, `run_*.py`, `find_*.R`, `*.Rmd`) → execute as-is and read the output\n  16: \n  17: Only follow this skill's re-analysis recipe below if **none** of the above exist. Re-running from raw data produces different numbers than the published answer and is much slower (often 5–10× turn count).\n  18: \n  19: ---\n  20: \n  21: ## BUSCO scogs questions (multi-species phylogenomics)\n  22: \n  23: data folders with `scogs_fungi.zip` and/or `scogs_animals.zip` ship\n  24: pre-computed per-ortholog alignments (and sometimes trees). The\n  25: question asks for a metric per group, or a Mann-Whitney U / median /\n  26: ratio comparison between groups.\n  27: \n  28: ### PRIMARY SCRIPT — both groups in one pass (use this FIRST)\n  29: \n  30: When the question compares animals vs fungi (Mann-Whitney U, ratio,\n  31: fold-change, paired difference), the bundled paired-comparison script\n  32: extracts both zips, computes the metric per ortholog for each group,\n  33: and emits ALL of: per-group summary, two-tailed Mann-Whitney U +\n  34: p-value (in both orderings since U is asymmetric), paired-ortholog\n  35: median diff, paired-ortholog median ratio, group-median ratio, and\n  36: lowest-non-zero ratios — in one run, no aggregation step needed:\n  37: \n  38: ```bash\n  39: python skills/tooluniverse-phylogenetics/scripts/scogs_paired_compare.py \\\n  40:     --data-folder \"$DATA_PATH\" --metric parsimony_informative\n  41: # Metrics: parsimony_informative, rcv, gap_percentage (alignment-only,\n  42: # Biopython-fast: ~2s for 500 alignments);\n  43: # treeness, dvmc, total_tree_length, evolutionary_rate, long_branch_score,\n  44: # patristic_distances (tree); treeness_over_rcv, saturation (both).\n  45: ```\n  46: \n  47: Output blocks (parse in Python or grep):\n  48: \n  49: ```\n  50: # SUMMARY group=animals: n=... mean=... median=... min=... max=... p25=... p75=... lowest_nonzero=... n_nonzero=...\n  51: # SUMMARY group=fungi:   n=... mean=... median=... min=... max=... p25=... p75=... lowest_nonzero=... n_nonzero=...\n  52: # MWU animals_vs_fungi: U=... p=...\n  53: # MWU fungi_vs_animals: U=... p=...        <-- U(a,b) + U(b,a) = n_a*n_b\n  54: # PAIRED n_common=N: median_diff(animals-fungi)=...  median_diff(fungi-animals)=...\n  55: # PAIRED RATIO median(animals/fungi)=... (n=...)    <-- for each common ortholog: a_val/b_val, then median\n  56: # PAIRED RATIO median(fungi/animals)=... (n=...)\n  57: # GROUP_MEDIAN_RATIO animals/fungi=...               <-- median(group_a) / median(group_b)\n  58: # GROUP_MEDIAN_RATIO fungi/animals=...\n  59: # GROUP_MEDIAN_DIFF animals-fungi=...\n  60: # LOWEST_NONZERO animals=... fungi=...\n  61: # LOWEST_NONZERO_RATIO animals/fungi=...\n  62: # LOWEST_NONZERO_RATIO fungi/animals=...\n  63: ```\n  64: \n  65: For `long_branch_score` and `patristic_distances` (multi-value-per-tree\n  66: metrics), pass `--per-tree-stat mean` or `--per-tree-stat median` to\n  67: choose the per-tree summary BEFORE the cross-tree MWU. The question\n  68: wording \"comparing **median** long branch scores\" means per-tree\n  69: summary = median; \"comparing **mean** long branch scores\" means\n  70: per-tree summary = mean. Run TWICE (once with each) if uncertain.\n  71: \n  72: ### Single-group script (when only one group is asked about)\n  73: \n  74: ```bash\n  75: python skills/tooluniverse-phylogenetics/scripts/scogs_phykit_pipeline.py \\\n  76:     --data-folder \"$DATA_PATH\" --group fungi --metric treeness --out /tmp/f.tsv\n  77: # Auto-falls-back to .faa.mafft when .faa.mafft.clipkit is absent\n  78: # (some scogs zips ship only mafft alignments, not clipkit trims).\n  79: ```\n  80: \n  81: ### `phykit parsimony_informative` is NOT a valid CLI subcommand\n  82: \n  83: PhyKIT's CLI exposes parsimony-informative-site count as\n  84: `parsimony_informative_sites` (alias `pis`). Calling\n  85: `phykit parsimony_informative <file>` returns the help banner with\n  86: non-zero exit and silently produces zero values. The bundled scripts\n  87: translate `parsimony_informative` → `parsimony_informative_sites`\n  88: automatically. The output is `<n_pi>\\t<n_total>\\t<percent>` — column\n  89: THREE is the percentage that questions usually ask for.\n  90: \n  91: ### Group-median ratio vs paired ratio (read this carefully)\n  92: \n  93: When a question phrases tree-length / RCV / DVMC comparisons as\n  94: \"ratio of fungal to animal X across orthologs\", there are TWO distinct\n  95: quantities:\n  96: \n  97: 1. **GROUP_MEDIAN_RATIO** = `median(values_fungi) / median(values_animals)`.\n  98:    Use ALL orthologs in each group independently. This is what\n  99:    group-comparison published numbers usually report (n_fungi can\n 100:    differ from n_animals, and \"across\" is a population statement, not\n 101:    a paired one).\n 102: \n 103: 2. **PAIRED RATIO median** = for each ortholog present in BOTH groups,\n 104:    compute `value_fungi / value_animals`, then take the median across\n 105:    common orthologs. Smaller denominator (intersection only) and a\n 106:    different number when the groups have different size.\n 107: \n 108: Default to GROUP_MEDIAN_RATIO unless the question explicitly says\n 109: \"matched ortholog\", \"paired ortholog\", \"per-ortholog ratio\", or \"for\n 110: each ortholog\". If the answer phrasing is ambiguous, BOTH numbers are\n 111: in the script's output — pick the one matching the question's\n 112: \"across\" / \"paired\" / \"ratio of medians\" phrasing.\n 113: \n 114: ### Total amino-acid count across single-copy orthologs — single representative, not all species\n 115: \n 116: When a BUSCO single-copy ortholog dataset (`single_copy_busco_sequences/`) is\n 117: present and the question asks \"**how many total amino acids** are present in all\n 118: single-copy ortholog sequences\", count **one representative sequence per\n 119: ortholog**, not the sum across all species/copies.\n 120: \n 121: Each `<ortholog_id>.faa` in `single_copy_busco_sequences/` typically contains\n 122: multiple species' copies of that ortholog (one each). Summing every sequence\n 123: across every species double/triple/N-fold counts each ortholog by the species\n 124: count and gives `n_species × correct_answer`.\n 125: \n 126: | Question phrasing | Count |\n 127: |---|---|\n 128: | \"total amino acids in all single-copy ortholog sequences\" | Sum of ONE sequence per ortholog (either the FIRST entry per file or the median-length entry) |\n 129: | \"total amino acids across N species' single-copy orthologs\" | Sum across species explicitly (multi-species sum) |\n 130: | \"average length of single-copy orthologs\" | Mean per-ortholog length (one per ortholog) |\n 131: \n 132: ❌ WRONG: `for f in *.faa: sum(len(rec.seq) for rec in SeqIO.parse(f, 'fasta'))` then sum across files (multi-species sum)\n 133: \n 134: ✅ RIGHT: `for f in *.faa: first_rec = next(SeqIO.parse(f, 'fasta')); total += len(first_rec.seq)` (one representative per ortholog)\n 135: \n 136: If your answer is `n_species × GT` (e.g. 32228 when GT looks like 13809 = 32228/2.33 ≈ 8 species × representative), you summed all species — re-do with one representative.\n 137: \n 138: ### Lowest-non-zero ratios\n 139: \n 140: For metrics that can legitimately equal 0 for highly conserved or\n 141: very short alignments (parsimony informative %, RCV on near-identical\n 142: seqs), \"lowest\" in a question typically means \"lowest non-zero\". The\n 143: paired script emits `LOWEST_NONZERO_RATIO` for both orderings — use\n 144: that line when the raw min in a group is 0.\n 145: \n 146: ### File-layout fallback (alignment naming)\n 147: \n 148: scogs zips ship in two shapes:\n 149: - **Full**: `<gene>.faa`, `<gene>.faa.mafft`, `<gene>.faa.mafft.clipkit`,\n 150:   `<gene>.faa.mafft.clipkit.treefile`, plus iqtree/bionj/log/mldist.\n 151: - **Alignment-only**: just `<gene>.faa` + `<gene>.faa.mafft`. No\n 152:   trees, no clipkit. Used for parsimony, RCV, gap-percentage\n 153:   questions. Use the `.faa.mafft` (NOT raw `.faa`) — the published\n 154:   metric was computed on the MAFFT-aligned file.\n 155: \n 156: Both bundled scripts auto-detect the layout and use the best available\n 157: alignment per ortholog. Do NOT re-run MAFFT or ClipKit yourself; the\n 158: shipped files are canonical.\n 159: \n 160: #### Which alignment goes with which metric (this changes the answer)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport urllib.request\nbase='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mims-harvard/ToolUniverse/main/skills/tooluniverse-phylogenetics/scripts/'\nfor fn in ['scogs_phykit_pipeline.py','scogs_paired_compare.py']:\n u=base+fn\n try:\n  data=urllib.request.urlopen(u,timeout=20).read().decode()\n  print('###',fn,'len',len(data))\n  open('/tmp/'+fn,'w').write(data)\n  print(data[:1000])\n except Exception as e: print('ERR',fn,e)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport urllib.request\nbase='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mims-harvard/ToolUniverse/main/skills/tooluniverse-phylogenetics/scripts/'\nfor fn in ['scogs_phykit_pipeline.py','scogs_paired_compare.py']:\n u=base+fn\n try:\n  data=urllib.request.urlopen(u,timeout=20).read().decode()\n  print('###',fn,'len',len(data))\n  open('/tmp/'+fn,'w').write(data)\n  print(data[:1000])\n except Exception as e: print('ERR',fn,e)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"### scogs_phykit_pipeline.py len 10391\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Run PhyKIT metrics on pre-computed BUSCO single-copy ortholog (SCOGs) trees.\n\nUsage:\n    python scogs_phykit_pipeline.py --data-folder /path/to/data \\\\\n        --metric treeness --group fungi\n    python scogs_phykit_pipeline.py --data-folder /path/to/data \\\\\n        --metric long_branch_score --group fungi --extra \"-v\"\n\nWhy this exists (RULE ZERO for BUSCO-orthology phylogenetics questions):\n    Some phylogenetics datasets ship with `scogs_fungi.zip` and\n    `scogs_animals.zip`, which contain the pre-computed alignments\n    (`*.faa.mafft.clipkit`) AND trees (`*.faa.mafft.clipkit.treefile`)\n    from the original analysis. The reference answers come from running\n    PhyKIT on THESE files. Re-running BUSCO → MAFFT → IQ-TREE from scratch\n    produces slightly different numbers (different IQ-TREE seed, ClipKit\n    decisions, MAFFT version) AND takes hours.\n\n    This script:\n      1. Auto-extracts scogs_<group>.zip into a workspace tmpdir if not\n         already ext\n### scogs_paired_compare.py len 21305\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Paired group comparison for BUSCO single-copy ortholog phylogenetics.\n\nThis script extracts BOTH `scogs_animals.zip` and `scogs_fungi.zip`,\ncomputes the requested per-ortholog metric on every ortholog, and emits\nboth per-group summaries AND the cross-group Mann-Whitney U + p-value\nin a single invocation. It exists to address timeouts the agent hits\nwhen re-running per-group batches separately.\n\nWhy this is faster than calling phykit_batch twice:\n\n- For metrics derivable from alignment columns alone (parsimony_informative,\n  rcv, gap_percentage), it uses Biopython directly — no subprocess fork\n  per file. ~100x speedup on 500 alignments.\n- For tree metrics (treeness, dvmc, long_branch_score, total_tree_length,\n  patristic_distances, evolutionary_rate), it spawns one phykit process\n  per file but extracts only ONCE per group. The agent had been re-running\n  full extraction per question.\n- It auto-falls-back across file naming variants — some scogs zips ship\n  on\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
